      en: "Shimba Hills National Reserve is a notable wildlife habitat in Kenya and ranks among the country's most geographically and culturally significant locations. Known as the last home of the rare sable antelope in Kenya. Its position close to long-established trade and migration routes has shaped its role in East African history and economy for many generations. Today the natural variety of the surrounding region is closely intertwined with local settlements, agriculture, and scientific research. For learners exploring the Plizio world, Shimba Hills National Reserve offers a clear example of how geography, climate, and culture interact in Kenya and influence the daily lives of its people. Geography and natural science K7–K8 — East African spaces and their functions."
